WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:05.749
With five previous Predator movies and dozens
of comics, 2018's The Predator had a wealth

00:00:05.749 --> 00:00:07.120
of material to draw from.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:11.680
Not surprisingly, some of that inspiration
hit the big screen in the form of Easter eggs.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.590
These little details may have been easy to
miss, but they point to some pretty crazy

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:17.640
shared connections in the Predator universe.

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:22.240
Buckle up, because we're taking a look at
all the small details you missed in The Predator.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:22.920
Spoilers ahead.

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:26.380
"Stick around."

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:28.430
A special spear

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:33.420
The two Alien vs. Predator films have always
been a source of contention among fans.

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:38.200
Some insist they're canon, while others say
they're little more than big budget fanfic.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:44.200
In 2004's Alien vs. Predator, the story is
that the predators were breeding the xenomorphs

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:47.030
on Earth to serve as the perfect game animals.

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:51.219
Near the end, one of the predators fashions
a spear out of a xenomorph tail and gives

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:54.940
it to the main protagonist, Alexa, so that
they can go hunt aliens together.

00:00:55.060 --> 00:01:00.200
When Casey Bracket first enters the Project
Stargazer lab in The Predator, she walks past

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:01.520
two display cases.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:06.620
One of them showcases several predator helmets
and a shoulder cannon, while the other contains

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:09.660
the xenomorph tail spear from Alien vs. Predator.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.160
Been here before

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:16.380
Once Casey is inside the main part of the
lab, Traeger hands a tablet that sums up everything

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.320
they know about the alien hunters.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:22.300
As she's flipping through the pictures, you
can catch a brief glimpse of scenes from the

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:23.820
first two Predator films.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:29.120
Traeger continues by saying that they've been
here before in '87 and '97.

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:31.960
Those dates refer to the first two movies.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.380
1987 is the year the first Predator was released.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:40.320
And although Predator 2 was released in 1990,
its story is set in 1997.

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:43.840
That's the obvious connection, but this reference
might go even deeper.

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:48.620
Starting in 1989, there have also been a ton
of Predator comic books and novelizations.

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:53.580
One of those was Predator: Kindred, which
began hitting stores in 1997.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:58.640
Released as a four-part series, Kindred details
a predator's arrival in a small, quiet town.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:03.640
Buddy Wilcox, a war vet who's slowly messing
up his family life, gets caught up in the

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:05.660
fight, while on the run from the police.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:10.500
That's basically Quinn McKenna in The Predator,
a war vet with a failing marriage who has

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:13.760
to fight the predator in a small town while
he's on the run from the government.

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:17.920
The comic even features panels that look a
lot like some of the scenes we see in the

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:20.320
film, even though the story ends up a little
different.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:24.860
Maybe it's all a coincidence, but if that's
Shane Black's idea of a nod to his story's

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:27.140
inspiration, it's definitely a subtle one.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:28.620
Family ties

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:33.780
Not content to simply mention past Predator
films, Shane Black also added at least one

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:36.300
solid connection to The Predator's predecessors.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:40.600
In the early lab scenes, we meet a bespectacled
scientist named Sean Keyes.

00:02:40.769 --> 00:02:45.040
He bites it pretty early when their captive
predator escapes and massacres the lab guys,

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:48.870
but if there's one thing we're sure of, it's
that his dad probably would have been proud

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:50.040
of the way he went out.

00:02:50.329 --> 00:02:55.859
In Predator 2, Gary Busey plays Peter Keyes,
a CIA agent who wants to capture the predator

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:56.580
alive.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:00.100
It doesn't work out, and he gets cut in half
for his troubles.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:04.559
Actor Jake Busey, who plays Sean Keyes, confirmed
in an interview that his character was indeed

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.290
the son of Peter Keyes.

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:11.480
He looks just like him, which isn't surprising
at all since Jake Busey is Gary Busey's real

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:11.980
son.

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:14.060
Tech upgrades

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:18.900
For all those call-backs to earlier films,
though, 2018's The Predator did make one smart

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:21.060
change to the spine-gatherers, their tech.

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:25.139
Sure, you could say that giving the aliens
better weapons, better ships, and better armor

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:26.820
gives the story a narrative boost.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:29.960
Things are supposed to be bigger and better
in the sequels.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:31.800
"number one.  the body count is always bigger."

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:35.060
"Number two, the death scenes are much more elaborate."

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:36.560
"More blood.  More gore."

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:38.280
"carnage candy"

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:42.059
But it also makes sense within the framework
of the Predator universe.

00:03:42.059 --> 00:03:46.559
After all, human technology has gotten a lot
better since the '80s, and it'd be a weird

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:51.739
twist if the predators hadn't made any technological
advances of their own in the past 30 years.

00:03:51.739 --> 00:03:56.370
Not even accounting for the upgraded predator,
2018's predator has plenty of little tweaks

00:03:56.370 --> 00:04:00.809
in his gear set that audience-members may
have missed, with everything from his bio-mask

00:04:00.809 --> 00:04:04.540
to his shoulder-mounted plasmacaster updated
for the modern day.

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:07.940
You won't be able to hide in the mud to get
away from this one, Arnold.

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:09.540
What's in a name?

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:14.459
It's not unusual for movies to place little
nods to the men and women working behind the

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:19.600
scenes, but The Predator manages to pull off
a big one, then hides it right in plain sight.

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:23.900
During the scenes at Rory's school, you can
clearly see the name of the institution both

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:28.840
above the front door and on the lit sign out
front: Lawrence A. Gordon Middle School.

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:33.319
That's a reference to producer Lawrence Gordon,
who worked on the first two Predator films,

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:34.630
as well as the 2018 one.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:39.539
In fact, Gordon has been part of the production
team on iconic movies since the '70s, producing

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:43.400
everything from Xanadu to Die Hard to the
upcoming Hellboy reboot.

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:49.340
He was also involved with 1979's The Warriors,
hence the school slogan: "Home of the Warriors."

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:54.740
Now, he's been immortalized forever as a school
that gets torn apart by a bloodthirsty alien.

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:56.160
Math lesson

00:04:56.820 --> 00:05:00.740
During the showdown between Quinn and Traeger
near the end of the film, things turn into

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:04.200
a brief standoff, with both sides hunkered
down behind vehicles.

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:08.530
In an attempt to defuse the situation, Traeger
shouts out for Quinn to surrender, saying

00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:11.200
he's outnumbered because "it's six vs. seven."

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:15.720
Immediately, a bullet flies, catching one
of Traeger's men in the head, and Quinn quips,

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:16.740
"Who taught you math?"

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:21.060
Like a lot of the movie, it's played for laughs,
but it's also a subtle callback to one of

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:24.060
Shane Black's previous movies, Kiss Kiss,
Bang Bang.

00:05:24.509 --> 00:05:29.180
In this detective comedy, Robert Downey Jr.
plays Harry Lockhart, a bumbling thief who

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:33.560
gets drawn into a murder mystery while taking
detective lessons from Val Kilmer's character.

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:37.500
Trying to get some information from a bad
guy, Harry puts a single bullet in a revolver

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:39.300
and pulls the trigger.

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:41.120
"Where is the girl"

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:44.040
What did you just do?"

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:48.760
Shocked, he tries to calculate the odds of
the bullet actually shooting on the first

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:49.260
try.

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:50.920
"You put a live round"

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:54.160
Yeah, there was an 8% chance... 8%?

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:56.220
"Who taught to math?"

00:05:56.380 --> 00:05:59.300
Guess the best jokes are the ones you repeat
over and over.

